{
  "about": "The declared backlog and its merge by the merge base (MERGETASKS1, LANDBACKLOG1; D87's LANDBACKLOG1 note): the table both twins hold, the merge tool's backlogOf and mergeByBase (tools/merge-branch.mjs) and the driver's BacklogMerge, which a landing's replay settles a backlog-only conflict by. Each `paths` row is the text of daoris.json (null where there is none) and the backlog read from it: none is TASKS.md; text that is not JSON, or JSON that is not an object, reads as no backlog (null), so nothing is settled; documents.backlog as text, or as an object's text `path`, is that path with each backslash a slash; anything else is TASKS.md. INTEGRATE1c: each `rows` row is the text of daoris.gates.json (null where there is none) and the files its `integration.merge` declares settle by rows (`by: \"rows\"`; the same merge by the base, line by line, as the backlog's), read by the merge tool's rowTablesOf and the driver's BacklogMerge.RowTablesOf: nothing where the text is not a JSON object, has no integration.merge list, or an entry has no non-blank text `paths`; each path trimmed with each backslash a slash, once, in the order first declared; an entry that is a `line` rule with a `run` writer, or has any other `by`, declares none (the writers wait for the accepted declaration, and no rule keeps one side's copy). INTEGRATE1d: both twins read these rows through the whole declaration's reader (integrationOf, IntegrationDeclaration.Read; `integration-declarations.json`), so a declaration in which that reader names any problem declares none, never a part. PROCSTEPS1a: each `merges` row is the base's, our side's and their side's lines (joined by LF; a line keeps its CR) and what the merge makes of them: a row marked `table` (a declared row table) by the merge base line by line (mergeByBase, BacklogMerge.ByBase), every other as the backlog, by its rows' ids (mergeByRows, BacklogRows.Merge). A row is a list line whose first bold token opens with an id (each `ids` row: the line, its key, the ids it covers, null ids where its list does not read); a row only one side added, removed or rewrote takes that side, one both changed alike (whitespace normalised: each run of spaces one, the line's end cut) is once, in our text, so a row one side archived leaves only where the other's copy is the base's or only moved, and every other is `differ` (its key and each side's text, null where removed), never merged word by word; a side covering an id twice is `twice`, one with a row whose ids do not read `unread`, refused before anything merges. The lines that are no row merge by the base, each row standing as its key, two adds at one place meeting unless both add rows alone. With the merged lines, or null; the lines kept from the hunks and rows taken and the base lines they dropped; and each base line left (1-based). Ours is the side the work lands on (main in the merge tool, the line in a replay), and two sides adding rows at one place keep ours first. Each `held` row is a made backlog held against those rules once a replay is made (rowsHeld, BacklogHeld.Of): the ids it covers twice, the rows it keeps that the rules leave out, those it lost that they keep, and whether the rules refuse its sides.",
